course: dictionary
lesson: 4343
title: The guesser
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A guesser is a person who gives an answer without knowing for sure. Guessing is not always bad. A good guesser uses clues and estimation, so the guess is often close to right. A random guesser picks by chance and often gets it wrong.
explain: In a test, a guesser can also be a rule or a player that picks an answer without really knowing. «Always choose the longest option» is a guessing rule: it may win sometimes, but it does not show that you understand the question.
explain: That is why a real answer should beat a guesser. If your score is no better than pure luck, you have not learned much yet. A lucky guesser may get one right, but a good answer comes from thinking, not chance.
explain: Computer helpers can be guessers too. «A simple next-word guesser counts which words came after a word in its text and picks the one it saw most often.» It does not know what the words mean; it only spots patterns. So check its answers, just as you check your own.
fact: A guesser is a person who gives an answer without knowing for sure.
fact: A good guesser uses clues and estimation, so the guess is often close to right.
fact: A random guesser picks by chance and often gets it wrong.
fact: In tests, a guesser can be a rule, such as always picking the longest option.
fact: «A next-word guesser counts which words came after a word and picks the one it saw most often.»
exercise: Sam says, «I'm a guesser at maths, I just pick.» What is a guesser? | *A person who gives an answer without knowing for sure | A person who never gives an answer at all | A teacher who marks the tests | A kind of maths sum
exercise: In a quiz, «always choose the longest option» is a rule. What kind of guesser is it? | *A rule that picks an answer without really knowing | A player who always wins | A clever way to show you understand | A type of sentence
exercise: «But every one of these helpers is a guesser.» What does this tell you about computer helpers? | They are people who never guess | They always tell the truth | *They can get things wrong, so check their answers | They cannot read words
test: What does a good guesser use to make a better guess? | *Clues and estimation | Pure luck only | Always the longest answer
test: Why should a real answer beat a guesser's score? | Because guessers always score zero | Because real answers are always longer | *Because a guesser's score is only what pure luck would give | Because guessing is against the rules of every test
exam: «A simple next-word guesser counts which words came after a word and picks the one it saw most often.» Why might this guesser pick the wrong word for a new sentence? | It counts too slowly to finish | It has never seen a word before in its life | *It only counts patterns and does not know what the words mean | It only picks words that are longer
